# M-AGREED-RETENTION'S FIRST ITEM, and panel 175's route E: a pointer C made,
# handed twice to a C function that frees it (2026-09-23).
#
# `consumes` on a `ptr` is refused (`unread_mark`), so this is the one spelling,
# and nothing in Heroes tracks it. Until panel 175 it died with ZERO bytes on
# Darwin, where libmalloc writes its reason only into a crash report, and on
# Windows, where the heap manager raises 0xC0000374; on Linux glibc printed its
# own line and Heroes nothing. The crash handler now says what it saw with no
# lease live: the signal, who raised it, and that this runtime did not.
#
# **What the case asserts is the one clause true on every platform**, that this
# runtime did not raise it. It does not pin the signal, which libmalloc splits
# between SIGTRAP and SIGABRT for one binary (the critic measured sixteen and
# four in twenty), nor the Heroes frame, which the walk cannot always find.
# Under `--sanitize` the report is ASan's `double-free` and ours is silent,
# because ASan's death callback raises the runtime's own flag.
#
# The same program over a handle is caught before C runs; that is the route a
# pointer the program must give back can take, `record Mem tag void`.

extern "fixedbugs-c-gives-a-pointer-back-twice-and-the-runtime-says-so.h"
    function make() -> ptr
    function release(p: ptr)

function main()
    p = make()
    release(p: p)
    release(p: p)
    print("not reached")
